    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the full test suite passes.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required defining membership level 4 as needing tenure of more than 365 days, while ensuring a newly created owner (zero tenure) can never exceed level 3.\n\nI made a focused change to `Owner.getMembershipLevel()` in `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java`:\n\n1. **Added a tenure factor** \u2014 the level now gains 1 when tenure exceeds 365 days, and the cap was raised from 3 to 4.\n2. **Extracted a small private helper** `getTenureDays()` \u2014 computes whole days from `registrationDate` to the current server date (`LocalDate.now()`, matching how `ValidateOwnerRegistrationDate` treats \"now\"), returning 0 when no registration date is recorded. Keeping this as its own single-responsibility method keeps `getMembershipLevel()` readable and the tenure concept reusable.\n3. **Updated the Javadoc** to reflect the 1\u20134 range and the tenure rule.\n\nI made the tenure helper **private** deliberately: the entity's public getters are serialized directly into the owner JSON, so a public `getTenureDays()` would have added an unintended `tenureDays` field to the response.\n\nBecause a new owner's `registrationDate` is stamped at creation, its tenure is 0 days \u2014 so the new-owner-with-email-and-no-namesake case lands at 1 + 1 (email) + 1 (namesakeCount 0) = **level 3**, never 4. The `coreNewOwnerCappedAtLevel3` acceptance test and the full suite are green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test, 0 failures).",
